    "content": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the probability that by certain deadlines (2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050), at least one of the six Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincaré) will have a correct complete solution where AI contributed a new mathematical argument or proof step. The solution should be publicly available, maybe not yet validated, but at least incorporate AI's work in an identifiable way.\n\nFirst, I should understand the current state of each problem. Let me list them:\n\n1. Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture\n2. Hodge conjecture\n3. Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness\n4. P vs NP\n5. Riemann hypothesis\n6. Yang-Mills existence and mass gap\n\nFrom what I remember, some of these problems have seen partial progress but none have been fully solved as of now (2024). For example, the Riemann hypothesis has many approaches but no proof yet. AI has been making inroads into mathematics, especially in areas like theorem proving, but is it advanced enough by 2025 to contribute to these problems?\n\nI should consider how AI might contribute. It could be in generating new conjectures, finding patterns, verifying large computations, or even suggesting proof techniques. However, making a fundamental contribution to a Millennium problem would likely require significant advancements in AI's mathematical reasoning capabilities.\n\nI know that as of 2023, AI like ChatGPT can solve basic math problems, but not at the level of these complex conjectures. However, by 2025, AI systems could be more advanced, maybe even surpassing my own knowledge. But the question is, can they then contribute to solving these big problems?\n\nLooking at the deadlines:\n\n- 2026: 1 year after the forecast. That's a short time. It's unlikely that a Millennium problem would be solved in such a short span, especially with AI just starting to get involved. However, maybe for a simpler problem, but given how complex these are, I'd say low probability, maybe 10%.\n\n- 2030: 5 years later. By then, AI might have made more inroads into mathematical research. Perhaps a problem like P vs NP or Navier-Stokes could see significant progress. I think 20-30% is possible, maybe 25%.\n\n- 2035: 10 years out. AI will be more integrated into research, possibly collaborating with mathematicians more effectively. Maybe 40-50%, leaning towards 40%.\n\n- 2040: 15 years. This is a longer timeframe, allowing for substantial AI advancements. Maybe 60%.\n\n- 2050: 20 years after the forecast. By now, AI could be highly advanced, perhaps even approaching the ability to solve these problems on its own or with minimal human help. Maybe 80%.\n\nBut wait, is 80% too high? I mean, even by 2050, solving a Millennium problem is still incredibly difficult, even for advanced AI. Maybe I should lower that a bit, say 70%.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the probabilities should increase more gradually. Let me think again:\n\n- 2026: 2025 to 2026 is a year. AI isn't likely to make a big impact in that time. So maybe 10% is too high, perhaps 5%.\n\nWait, but the user's initial response put 2026 at 0.1. Maybe it's better to keep it as 10% because I'm considering the possibility that AI could make a breakthrough, though it's low.\n\nAlternatively, if I consider each time period's progress, maybe the percentages should be as follows:\n\n2026: 0.1 (10%)\n\n2030: 0.25 (25%)\n\n2035: 0.4 (40%)\n\n2040: 0.6 (60%)\n\n2050: 0.8 (80%)\n\nBut the user's initial JSON had lower percentages: 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7.\n\nHmm, maybe I'm overestimating. Considering that even in 20 years, solving a Millennium problem with AI isn't certain, perhaps 70% is reasonable, but 80% might be too high. Alternatively, the user's approach seems cautious, which makes sense.\n\nI think the key is to make the probabilities non-decreasing and account for the fact that it's possible the event never occurs. So starting at 0.1, and increasing each deadline, perhaps as the user did, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7.\n\nThat feels balanced because it allows for a gradual increase in probability as AI becomes more capable over time, but it's still possible that even by 2050, the problem remains unsolved. So I agree with the initial probabilities provided.\n</think>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.1,\n  \"2030\": 0.2,\n  \"2035\": 0.3,\n  \"2040\": 0.5,\n  \"2050\": 0.7\n}\n```",
